ge  and so then going back to  you know staying within the idea of  source um  right now um cbd is  legal to be sold within the state of  south carolina  but again um it it's one of those things  that's just sort of  taken off and it's everywhere it's it's  in every  grocery store and every um gas station  i mean it's you know some retail stores  you can buy it online  and so then it becomes so the next  question is you know how do we get  a reliable source or how do we know that  that source is reliable  and as consumers  we're not yet sophisticated enough to  make that determination it's kind of  like when i  walk into the grocery store and the  the wine aisle i mean i i tend to go for  something with a pretty label and maybe  a price  point you know maybe not the cheapest  but certainly not the most expensive and  so  um cbd as you know as  a consumer i i kind of think that that's  where we are at this point it's like oh  well which one has the  the most appeal um because there's  little known about  what um the contents are in that bottle  um it's it's a self-regulated market and  some products are good  and reputable and um others  are um you know don't necessarily have  contain what they um what they say they  contain  as far as the label um so  that is definitely a challenge um and  then we've had  instances um i have been contacted  previously by parents  who actually um used  cbd for their children and  bss was called on them and i know of at  least one case  where a child was taken from from his  mother  um for using cbd oil he had chronic  migraine  um and the school noticed he was he  missed a lot of school i guess and the  school noticed that he was  he was doing better and he was attending  school and they asked if he was taking  in any new medication that was helping  he said yeah i'm taking cbd oil and i  guess  they had a concern called dss and dss  when they came  you know the parent cooperated she  didn't see anything wrong with it  he was thriving he was his health had  improved  um and like i said again it's legal here  um but was unfamiliar with what cbd was  do did a quick google search and found  out it was  it came from marijuana and her  understanding of what she read was that  this mom was giving her child  marijuana and they took the child into  dss  custody she did get her child back but  um this was a single mom with limited  resources  to actually have epilepsy herself and  that's why she had  cbd in her home in with  um you know didn't work a full-time job  because of her  medical condition um so it was it was a  challenge and it's scary when you think  okay this is a fully  product but there isn't this vast  knowledge of  um what is legal and what is not legal  and there are no protections under the  law  at this time um regarding things like  see you know like um dealing with dss  so that's just the cbd  portion now when you get into  whole plant that is not legal at all in  our state  and so right now our biggest challenge  is moving the legislature to  get to a point where they see and  understand the benefit  or the potential benefit of having a  safe and regulated market so that we do  have  access to products that are tested  and labeled and this interaction with  our physicians in consultation with our  physicians and monitoring by our  physicians  so that's the ultimate goal so i know  that was a lengthy question but there  was a lot  in there that i felt like was important  to share  because i think so many states have  legal access now that  um many of them may take it for granted  and  they kind of forget what it was like it  may not have been  been aware like prior to um having just  like  that provided protections for patients  so jill how did you get involved in your  advocacy efforts  um well when my daughter was  six years old she had she was really  struggling  with her seizures she's very high  frequency so 800 to 1 000 seizures a day  easily  and we had tried a multitude of  