pharmaceutical drugs  combinations we did the ketogenic diet  we had seen specialists up and down the  east coast  and we just weren't getting any answers  and  our her neurologist actually reached a  point where he said you know i just i've  got nothing else for you i really have  nothing else  and we had seen the sanjay gupta weed  story  um where a little girl in colorado had  gotten access to cbd that's how i  learned about it and he said you know  it's worth looking into  and when we did what we found was that  we couldn't get it in south carolina  um and if we wanted to get it in  colorado  you couldn't just fly out there and get  it and bring it home  because you know even though now it's  being sold nationwide  um under hemp um as a hemp  product back then the the federal farm  bill  um legalizing the cultivation of hemp  had just been passed  and state legislature legislatures were  just starting to get caught up with that  so nobody was actually really growing it  um at that point so these were all cbd  products that were derived from  marijuana  and so this was very early on  and so you couldn't just go to colorado  and get it and bring it home because  that would be considered drug  trafficking  if you went there for treatment you  actually had to move there  um which means you you couldn't really  come back  and a lot of a lot of people in south  carolina made that choice because they  had children who were very very ill  and they were desperate and they  separated themselves from their families  and moved out there and that just wasn't  really a good option for me  so i was actually fortunate that my  mother-in-law lived down in beaufort and  had a mutual friend with the state  senator down there  and she had she was talking to this  friend about  her granddaughter my daughter and um  he said well you know i have a colleague  in my law practice who happens to be a  state state senator you know maybe  there's something he could do to help  out  so they met and then we met and  he did some research and found that  there was an old  law on the books regarding from 1980  regarding  legalizing marijuana for certain  conditions  to be administered through our dhak  which is the department of health and  environmental control  so he kind of took that at the bare  bones of that  and created um a cbd bill that basically  just legalized  cbd oil for patients with epilepsy  provided they had a doctor's certified  letter stating that they did have severe  epilepsy  and had failed multiple pharmaceuticals  and that they might benefit from the use  of cbd oil  so my mother-in-law and i spent a lot of  time  up in columbia where our state house is  located we met with lawmakers we wrote  letters  we testified we met the governor's  office  and at the time our lawmakers really  didn't know a whole lot about it but  they kind of just said well if it helps  sick children  how can we not support it right so the  bill got passed  rather quickly but the problem was once  it passed  and i got my daughter her certified  letter  um i was still empty-handed because  you know we didn't actually legalize  anything we just said something wasn't  illegal in certain circumstances yeah so  it didn't provide us  with access um and so  that was um an eye-opener for me  and also for a lot of people across the  state who  um i had in the meantime i had actually  started a facebook group which is the  name of my organization  south carolina compassionate care  alliance and  i started this facebook group because i  wanted to provide  other parents and other patients across  the state with information hey  this cbd bill passed but here's what it  is here's what it isn't  because people don't have a good general  understanding of how the legislative  process works  and they also didn't have a complete  understanding of what cbd was  they just thought it was marijuana and  so i would have patients who said i work  in the food and  beverage industry and if it's legal can  i just  get something from one of my co-workers  and give it to my child and i'm like no  um so that's really where the the  background um  started you know was with the cbd bill  and  um again i've just i found in my years  of doing this that  people don't know who their lawmakers  are  they don't know who to reach out to um  they're concerned that if they are using  something illegally now that if they  tell their lawmaker that that they're  going to get turned in  and that's really not the case i i found  that to be  quite the opposite that the lawmakers  are very understanding and they keep  those conversations confidential  and they are very compassionate people  and so  i feel like it's my job because i've  been doing this as long as i have to  continue to educate individuals  on on how to do that  wow what an amazing story  um well what can parents do now  to assure that they're getting safe  good quality products for their children  well um like i said obviously in the  beginning especially that was a real  challenge  and we were very desperate  um we had children who were suffering  and it's very difficult as a mom to  watch your child  suffer so we networked through  other parents and other parents in other  states and friends and friends of  friends  and got access to whatever we could get  our hands on  and this time at this time early on  these products were all black market so  um one thing i would urge  parents to use extreme caution  with the the sources that they're  utilizing  because we were sold products that we  didn't even know if they were fit for  human consumption let alone whether they  were going to benefit our children  and that was just just a risk that we  you know that we had to take um  because we were desperate and we wanted  to help our kids um  so you know one thing that i would  urge is to make sure you have a reliable  source  so how do you do that because cbd is  everywhere now  um like i said earlier it's on the  internet it's at the grocery store it's  at the gas station  um one thing you can do  is i would do i would do a pretty  thorough google search  on your manufacturer and find out a lot  of these companies are white labeling  and a lot of them all have the same  source with a different company name on  them  um so find out who you know who their  source is  make sure it's um you know it's  domestically  cultivated and also make sure that  there's a certificate of analysis  and if a company is not willing to put  that la that's and what that is is  actually it's a laboratory it's a  third-party laboratory test  um and so if companies aren't willing to  put their laboratory test um and it's  usually  by batch and the bottle will have a  label  with a barcode on it just like any you  know product you purchase  and that bar code will match that batch  will match  exactly with the the lab report for that  product  um so that is really you know that's the  best assurance and then like i said make  sure that the company  is reputable um ask around ask  other parents ask you know  what what other parents are using  because word of mouth is also  a great a great tool and a great  resource because  when we when we first started out we  were the very first cbd oil i gave my  daughter was called  sunflower power and all it said on it  was 20 to 1 cbd  well i didn't even that sounded good  somebody told me that's what you needed  for epilepsy was 20 to 1.  i don't know and but now i know 20 to 1  doesn't really mean anything because you  have to know what the one is  you know what the 20 is so  um you know it's just um you know it was  a learning curve and you know like i  said i mean even  you know we we had an occasion where we  were sold products that were labeled  um that had no we found out later  through  laboratory testing that they had no  traceable amounts of cbd in them  whatsoever  and those companies are out there um  they're out there to make a buck  and you know fortunately the fda has  cracked down  on some of those companies um that have  um  false labeling um but you know with so  many companies out there now  it's really difficult to um  to you know for the fda to keep up with  all of them and track them down if  they're  if they're bad players um but since this  is not regulated by the fda  this is a self-regulated market some  companies  have higher standards and are assured  making sure that the products they are  producing  are what they say they are that they are  safe that they do  utilize third-party laboratory testing  that you know there are no residual  solvents or molds or  microbes that could actually cause  damage to patients  um making sure that those are not  present or not present at levels where  they could potentially cause harm  so um being an educated consumer is  really  vital in this market that's  that's a tremendous  set of comments and guidelines that all  parents should follow it's critically  important  to know that the company that you're  using  is actually legitimate there's some  estimates  that there are over three thousand  companies  providing hemp based products in the  united states today  if you had a crystal ball what do you  foresee in the near future  regarding medical cannabis in south  carolina  and you know i was uh privileged to  attend the la  the last medical university at south  carolina's conference that  that you uh put on  um and got to meet and spend a little  bit of time  with one of your state senators tom  davis  um give us an idea of how you see  uh you know his uh efforts in  champion legalization and  and your experience in in this space  today  well um i i pretty much threw the  crystal ball out the window last week  when we all got put on shot  shut down because of the coronavirus  [Laughter]  um if you asked me that question a week  or two ago i might have had a a  more articulate answer but um  today i i i don't know that the crystal  ball  theory is going to be really helpful um  and i always do say  because i've done this as long as i have  that i just can't predict what's going  to happen at the state house  crazy things happen i mean one day we  thought our bill was going to be heard  and pass on the floor  and it happened to be clemson day and  um so the entire you know floor was  filled with orange and everyone was  you know celebrating clemson and um  so they basically ditched the calendar  for the day and then the bill didn't  pass till the next day but you know so  crazy things happen um a pandemic is not  something i could have possibly  predicted  so um i know that senator davis has  worked  very very hard this session  in first of all drafting a  really phenomenal phenomenal bill um  to on the get-go and you know after a  few few years of this and listening to  concerns  and listening to advocates and listening  to stakeholders  um and looking at what other states are  doing um he's he  he started out with a really terrific  bill but  then you know there's still the  naysayers there's still those who  you know want to proceed with even more  caution who want it narrower  and so he has sat down with so many  different groups with the south carolina  medical association and law enforcement  and victims advocates and religious  groups and i mean literally everyone who  has something to say  on this piece of legislation and he has  taken that into account  and he has you know and and and there  are complaints from  the advocates you know from the  grassroots folks saying this feels so  narrow it's not going to help anyone  but he knows what he needs to do to get  that bill  through the senate once the bill gets  through the senate  um we've got the support and the ability  to get it passed in the house it's just  going to have to be very very tightly  narrowed  in the senate and so i mean he  he knows he knows what he needs to do  and he's working that plan um  unfortunately  in fact we were supposed to have a  hearing on the bill today in the senate  and senate medical affairs committee  and of course um that got cancelled  uh we were all supposed to have a  hearing next week on the house version  of the bill  also canceled so it's a little hard now  to predict i'm hopeful  that they will come back into session  and  you know see the need to go ahead and  get this thing  moved because you know we are in a  health crisis  and um you know i  you know it's i have no idea whether you  know cannabinoid therapy would in any  way  help um with the coronavirus but  certainly with  people's health in general um you know  obviously we knew there are benefits  so it's very possible that they could  um do extend the session into the summer  and finish things up  or it may be a situation where we have  to start a new  um in january but you know that just  sort of remains to be seen at this point  well we do know that  is one of the important physiologic  systems that do protect us  against infection and so  just theoretically it would make sense  that  if our endocannabinoid system was in  good shape  we would be able to weather a viral  infection  much better than those that had an  endocannabinoid  deficiency syndrome  that makes sense well  how can parents patients and other  supporters get involved in your advocacy  efforts  to help bring safe access to cannabis  products for patients  like your daughter  that's another great question so like i  said we do have an organization sc  compassionate care alliance we are  a charitable organization um we're  registered with the secretary of state  and we're in the process of finally  finalizing our 501c3 paperwork for the  irs  so we um we do accept donations  and financial support goes a long way  for any non-profit as you can imagine  we're very small  but we do a tremendous amount of work  our involvement with the musc  annual medical cannabis update  conference where we bring in  speakers from all over the united states  now  that is a huge huge benefit  to the medical professionals within our  state as well as  as interested parties it's a cme cle  accredited  conference and so that's part of our  work that we've been doing  we also sent several senators from the  medical affairs committee to maryland so  they can see firsthand what  a very high tech  cannabis medical cannabis cultivation  and processing facility and dispensary  look like  because i feel like they still have so  many questions that were unanswered  and seeing it firsthand made a huge  difference so that's where a lot of our  funds have gone in the last years really  you know like i said that education  piece  but for individuals um i i  cannot stress enough the single most  important thing you do you can do  is contact your lawmaker and tell your  story  and tell them why it's important to you  that  they um that they support the  compassionate care act  um and there is a link on our website  which  is www.sdcompassion.com  where they can look up their lawmakers  or they can go to  www.scstatehouse.gov  and click on find your legislator and  then you put in your address and it goes  straight to who your lawmaker is  we also have a click to advocate button  on our website so you can  click that button and it sends a letter  directly to your lawmaker  but i still recommend following up with  an in-person meeting or a phone call  um just so they they can hear your hear  your voice  and understand your passion and your  need for medical cannabis  you don't have to be an expert you don't  have to be an expert on the bill you  don't have to be an expert on the  science  you know you you know your personal  story  and that's really what resonates with  lawmakers  so there are scientists that are  physicians there are researchers  that are also meeting with lawmakers so  let them do their job you do your job as  a patient and as a supporter of the bill  and and share your perspective because  that's really what  um what lawmakers are looking for from  their constituents  that's tremendous advice  that all of our viewers take that to  heart  the voice of voters really does make a  difference  and when it's personalized it even makes  more of an impact as as jill  and and i have experienced uh in in the  states that i  operate in yeah and i was actually going  to say  that that um that's important you know  that's every bit as important in a state  that has a legal  program now because of course there's  always there's always a need for  improvement  no state has a perfect program yet um so  be involved and and unfortunately as  as gross as it kind of seems be  politically involved  um you know we are south carolina is a  red state and i've personally gotten  very  involved in the local charleston county  republican party  and this is a topic that i bring up  quite often  at meetings we have had medical cannabis  panels at some of the republican party  meetings  and i spearheaded the adoption of a  resolution by the charleston county gop  in support of the medical cannabis bill  and none of that  would have happened if i hadn't been you  know really involved  in in pushing this issue um  in within my county um so you know  it it really does make a tremendous  impact if you can  if you can show up show up to these  meetings  talk about medical cannabis and um and  you know and tell your story  great guidance  well i'd like to thank jill for her  informative answers and thank you to our  wonderful audience today  for your thought-provoking questions you  may continue  submitting questions and our speakers  will follow up via email  that's all for now thank you for joining  us and we'll see you next time  thank you
 

